WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.441
Structured input appears in records,
delimited fields, key-value files,

2
00:00:04.521 --> 00:00:07.461
headers, simple messages, and
configuration formats.

3
00:00:07.541 --> 00:00:11.356
The program should parse structure before
trusting individual fields.

4
00:00:11.436 --> 00:00:15.315
A value is not meaningful until the parser
knows which field it belongs to,

5
00:00:15.395 --> 00:00:19.648
whether the record is complete, and
whether the surrounding structure is valid.

6
00:00:19.728 --> 00:00:23.043
Required and optional fields should be
validated separately.

7
00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:25.375
A required field must be present and
valid.

8
00:00:25.455 --> 00:00:29.146
An optional field may be absent, but if it
appears, it still needs type,

9
00:00:29.226 --> 00:00:31.040
length, range, and format checks.

10
00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.560
Defaults should be explicit and should not
hide malformed input.

11
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.080
Duplicate fields need intentional
handling.

12
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.287
Some formats may allow repeated fields,

13
00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:43.870
some may treat the last value as
authoritative, and others should reject duplicates.

14
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.454
The program should not accidentally accept
whichever value happens to be parsed last

15
00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:50.348
if that is not part of the contract.

16
00:00:50.428 --> 00:00:53.618
Field-level validation should match each
field's meaning.

17
00:00:53.698 --> 00:00:57.014
A name, count, size, flag, path segment,
identifier, timeout,

18
00:00:57.094 --> 00:01:00.346
and message type are different even if
they all arrive as text.

19
00:01:00.426 --> 00:01:04.805
Cross-field consistency may also matter
when one field controls the length, type,

20
00:01:04.885 --> 00:01:06.386
or allowed range of another.

21
00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:08.843
Safe rejection is part of parser
discipline.

22
00:01:08.923 --> 00:01:12.364
If a record is incomplete, ambiguous,
oversized, inconsistent,

23
00:01:12.444 --> 00:01:14.195
or outside the accepted grammar,

24
00:01:14.275 --> 00:01:18.403
the parser should return a predictable
error and avoid partial state updates.

25
00:01:18.483 --> 00:01:23.050
Deeper code should receive either a
validated representation or no representation at

26
00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:23.380
all.
